course: dictionary
lesson: 9725
title: Sunburnt and staying safe in the sun
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sometimes a word tells you a story. Think about what happens when you stay in strong sunshine too long. «If you get sunburnt, you get out of the sun, cool the skin with a cool shower, drink water and tell an adult.» The sentence gives you the clues: get out of the sun, cool the skin, drink water. So sunburnt must mean that your skin has been burnt by the sun.
explain: The meaning also tells you what it feels like: your skin is red, sore and hot. Sunburnt is an adjective when it describes skin, as in «People with darker skin can still get sunburnt.» It is also the past form of the verb sunburn, so you can say «He sunburnt his nose at the beach.»
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «People with darker skin can still get red, sore, hot skin from the sun and still need sun protection.» It still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
fact: Sunburnt means your skin is red, sore and hot because you were in strong sunshine too long.
fact: Sunburnt is also the past form of the verb sunburn, and sunburned is another correct spelling.
fact: If you get sunburnt, get out of the sun, cool the skin with cool water, drink and tell a grown-up.
fact: Sun cream, a hat, shade and clothing all protect your skin from the sun.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, people remember to protect their skin with the saying Slip, Slop, Slap.
exercise: «If you get sunburnt, you get out of the sun.» What does sunburnt mean? | *Skin that is red, sore and hot from too much sun | Skin that is cold and wet from the rain | Skin that is covered in mud | Skin that has been painted
exercise: «Pale pigs can get sunburnt, so farmers sometimes give them shade or sun cream.» Why do farmers give pigs shade? | To make the pigs grow taller | To help the pigs sleep at night | To stop the pigs from eating | *To protect the pigs' skin from the sun
exercise: Which word is another spelling of sunburnt? | sunny | sunshine | *sunburned | sunbed
test: «Her nose was sunburnt after the long day at the beach.» What does sunburnt suggest about her nose? | It is much longer than usual | *It is red and sore from the sun | It is covered in sand and shells | It is cold and wet from the sea
test: In Australia and New Zealand, which saying reminds people to protect their skin from the sun? | *Slip, Slop, Slap | Stop, Look, Listen | Hop, Skip, Jump | Ready, Steady, Go
exam: Tom says «People with darker skin can still get sunburnt.» What should he remember? | *Anyone can get sunburnt, so everyone needs sun protection | Sunburn only happens in winter | Only pale people get sunburnt | Darker skin never needs sun protection
